  "explain": {
    "text": "The governor and the AVR are the two basic controls on every set. The AVR trims the field to hold the voltage. The governor trims the fuel to hold the speed, and so the frequency. A sudden load slows the set first. The governor then adds fuel to catch it. Where the speed then settles is something you set.\n\nOn its own, a set makes whatever power its load draws. The alternator pulls back on the shaft with a torque equal to power ÷ speed. The engine's torque must match it. Any gap speeds up or slows the spinning mass. Add 126 kW to a set turning at 1800 rev/min, which is 188.5 rad/s. The load's torque rises by 126 000 ÷ 188.5 = 668 N m at once. The fuel cannot follow that fast. For a moment the engine falls short. Take an example shaft carrying 20 kg m² of inertia. The set slows at 668 ÷ 20 = 33.4 rad/s². Say the governor takes 0.1 s to answer. By then the set has lost 3.34 rad/s. That is 1.8% of its speed. The frequency follows the speed. So by 0.1 s it has fallen 1.8% too, from 60 to 58.9 Hz. This fall is a [[frequency dip]]. Then the governor adds fuel. The speed goes on falling until the engine's torque matches the load's again. Only then does it climb back.\n\nWhere it climbs back to depends on the governor. A governor that brings the speed back exactly to its set point at any load is called [[isochronous]]. The other kind lowers its own target speed as it opens the fuel. More load needs more fuel, so the speed settles lower. That fall is its [[droop]]. Droop is the percentage change in speed that takes the governor through its full travel, from no load to full load.[^2] With 5% droop, full load runs at 100% of rated speed and no load at 105%.[^3] Take a 60 Hz set with 4% droop. Its droop in hertz is 4% of 60 Hz, which is 2.4 Hz. So no load runs at 62.4 Hz and full load at 60 Hz. A straight line joins them. Half load is 252 kW of 504 kW. There the set runs at 62.4 − 2.4 × 0.5 = 61.2 Hz. The set point slides the whole line up or down without tilting it.\n\nDroop is set on purpose. When sets run side by side, speed control is what shares the kW between them. On a set running alone, droop has a cost. Its frequency moves with the load. If all the load trips, the set settles at the top of its line. That is above its rated frequency. These figures come from a simplified picture. It uses a fixed response time and example values for the inertia. **The governor trims the fuel to hold the speed. Isochronous brings the speed back exactly. Droop lets it settle a set few per cent lower from no load to full load.**"
  },
  "analogy": {
    "text": "Think of a battery with some internal resistance. Its terminal voltage is its open-circuit voltage minus the current × r. That is a straight line falling with load. The open-circuit voltage plays the set point, and r plays the droop. A regulated supply that holds its voltage at any current plays the isochronous governor. Put two cells in parallel. Their open-circuit voltages and slopes decide how they share the current.",
    "breaks": "A cell's slope is a real loss inside it, which turns into heat. A governor's droop wastes nothing. It is only a setting in the control. And a cell answers a load at once. A set dips first, because its fuel must catch up with a spinning mass."
  },
  "practice": {
    "text": "**Working out a droop line**\n\n- Find the droop in hertz: the droop % × the rated frequency ÷ 100.\n- If the set runs at its rated frequency on full load, its no-load frequency is the rated frequency plus the droop in hertz.\n- At any load, the frequency is the no-load frequency − the droop in hertz × the load ÷ the rated load.\n- Find the fall at a chosen load: the droop in hertz × that load ÷ the rated load. To run at a chosen frequency there, set the no-load set point to that frequency plus the fall.\n- For a sudden load, find the torque gap: the kW added × 1000 ÷ the speed in rad/s. The set slows at that gap ÷ the inertia until the fuel catches up.\n\n**Rules of thumb:** a sudden load dips the frequency before the governor can catch it. A set alone on droop runs high on light load. It sits at its rated frequency only at the load its set point was chosen for.\n\n**Worked example:** a 50 Hz set rated 400 kW has 3% droop and runs at 50 Hz on full load. Its droop in hertz is 3 × 50 ÷ 100 = 1.5 Hz. So no load sits at 51.5 Hz. A load of 240 kW is 0.6 of its rating. There it runs at 51.5 − 1.5 × 0.6 = 51.5 − 0.9 = 50.6 Hz. To run it at 50 Hz on 240 kW, set its no-load point to 50 + 0.9 = 50.9 Hz.\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Treating droop as a fault. A set on droop that settles below its no-load frequency is doing what it was set to do.\n- Taking the droop % as hertz. 4% of 60 Hz is 2.4 Hz, not 4 Hz.\n- Expecting the governor to stop the dip. A governor that measures speed adds fuel only once the speed has started to fall. Then the engine takes time to answer.\n- Forgetting that a load trip sends a set on droop above its rated frequency."
  },
